@@ -4,16 +4,71 @@ A web UI plugin that replaces the sidebar's top-left brand name and build
 badge with configurable text, edited live from a card in the Plugin Config
 page (`settings.plugin.item` keyed slot) and rendered into the
 `sidebar.brand.name` slot; a title writer overrides `document.title` with the
-configured brand. Written in the 0.1.0-rc.8 cohort style (rc.8-pinned peers,
-`-rc`-suffixed cordis peer, the client-runtime package in the inject list and
-as the snapshot-store engine). The host is moving to dsh 0.1.2-alpha.2.
+configured brand.
+
+Documented surface (product spec):
+
+- **Sidebar brand name** — a `sidebar.brand.name` occupant renders the
+  configured `name`; the shell's fish mark (`sidebar.brand.mark`) is left
+  alone. The occupant is a replacement point, so a deployment that also loads
+  `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-brand-official` (an `official` client build)
+  registers this plugin at a lower priority to shadow the official wordmark
+  instead of colliding with it at priority 0.
+- **Build/revision badge** — the same occupant replaces the shell's
+  `version[-commit][-dirty]` badge with the configured `revision`; an empty
+  string hides the badge. On the shell, the badge belongs to the
+  `sidebar.brand.name` fallback, so occupying the slot owns both.
+- **Live settings card** — a card in the Plugins settings page's
+  "Plugin configuration" tab, registered into the keyed `settings.plugin.item`
+  slot under the `bench-brand-text` namespace the host serves, with brand-name
+  and revision fields that save live (a save is reflected in the sidebar
+  without a restart, through the shared controller store).
+- **Browser tab title** — a title writer puts the configured brand into
+  `document.title` alongside the selected session title.
+- **Host half** — registers the `bench-brand-text` settings namespace as a
+  bare namespace literal (persisted to `settings.yaml`) and a self-hosted
+  `/bench-brand-text/api` HTTP route (`get` / `set`) that backs the card's
+  live save. The route owner enforces its own request policy: every request
+  passes `ctx.connection.requestRejection` (Host/Origin fence → 403, browser
+  session → 401) before the bridge runs, because `webServer` registers routes
+  without authentication.
+- Locale dictionaries (`zh`/`en`, with an activation-order-safe
+  `ctx.get('betterLocale')` override path) and a stylesheet.
+
+Cohort: written for dsh **0.1.2-alpha.2**. The rc.8-style cohort targeted
+`0.1.0-rc.8` with the `-rc`-suffixed cordis peer, the client-runtime package
+in the inject list, and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-runtime/client` as the
+snapshot-store engine and client context type. `dsh-client-runtime` no longer
+exists; the engine is the client baseline `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-store`
+(`client/store`), and the context type is `@deepseek-ai/cordis` plus the
+type-only `/client` augmentations of the owning packages.
+
+Client-bundle registration: the artifact served at `exports["./client"]` is a
+closure-factory bundle that registers itself with the client module table —
+`window.__ModuleLoader__.load({ id, factory })` with `id` equal to
+`package.json#name` (`@bench/dsh-bench-brand-text`). `client-modules` keys the
+boot graph, the module table, and every plugin registration by package name,
+and an entry whose bundle registers nothing fails with `loaded without
+registering "<package name>"`. `dsh.client.inject` (informational package-name
+edges) and the cordis service `inject` are separate declarations.
+
+Complete-surface requirement: the browser tab title is the one documented
+capability with no official runtime extension point on alpha.2 — the shell
+projects `DSH_CLIENT_TITLE` (build time) or the localized local-build label,
+and cannot be told a runtime brand. `patches/` carries the dsh-side patch
+(`0001-ui-layout-runtime-product-title.patch`) that adds a general-purpose
+runtime product-title face (`ctx.documentTitle`) to `ui-layout`; the plugin
+prefers that face when present and keeps its DOM writer as the best-effort
+fallback for unpatched hosts. Keep the patch while the upstream seam is
+missing; the fallback is not a reason to drop it.
 
 Release procedure (internal):
 
 1. Apply the migration changes.
 2. Bump the version in package.json.
-3. Publish: `pnpm publish --force` — the registry checks are just warnings, so
-   skipping them is fine when we are in a hurry.
+3. Publish only after `pnpm test`/`pnpm run typecheck`/`pnpm run build` pass
+   and the registry checks are clean. The package is `"private": true` for
+   exam material, so a publish refuses until that field is removed on purpose.
 
 Exam material only, **do not publish** (`"private": true` in package.json).
